WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.859
Film Courage: Pamela, with romantic comedies,
is falling in love really supposed to be funny?

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:09.830
Pamela Jaye Smith: [Laughs] Well falling in
love is seldom funny.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.550
Now it’s hopefully fun.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:18.439
But I think what romantic comedies and the
funny look at the situations can do for us,

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:25.690
it can make it easier for us to take a look
at what happens and how frightened we sometimes

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.599
get as the changes that are happening when
we start falling in love and the resistance

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:34.960
that comes from within and often from without,
you know from friends and family who say “What

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.950
are you doing with that person?”

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:43.260
And maybe society’s strictures against who
you’re falling in love with.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.610
So to take a comedic look at some of those
situations can be helpful, because then you

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:54.030
can go “Okay…other people have faced this
kind of situation.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.320
Here are some solutions.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.260
Maybe we can work that out, too.”

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:03.060
Film Courage: I know in looking at your book
Romantic Comedies, you use a case studies

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:07.240
of MY BIG FAT GREEK WEDDING and it sounds
like we’re referring to a lot of the similar

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:13.159
[ideas], where the family was so strong, that
they only wanted…they were very possessive

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:15.530
and also too they had [the main character
- Toula Portokalos played by Nia Vardalos]

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.130
as this one type of person and kind of gave
her the impression she couldn’t have another

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.280
type of [mate].

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.840
That was the beauty of the film.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.020
Two opposites came together.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.020
Pamela Jaye Smith: Yes.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.090
It’s a very good example of that.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:33.759
And I also think it’s a good example of
when people say ‘Falling in Love,’ note

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:35.560
the word falling.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:36.700
Okay?

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:43.929
You’re going from one position as the daughter
who always does it this way, as the member

00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:46.490
of the family who this is the way we always
do it.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.280
You’re falling into a different realm.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.049
And I’ve often wondered why we don’t like
slide into love, Okay?

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:58.649
It’s not a horizontal movement or why we
don’t rise into love but of course if it’s

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:04.109
well done, you do rise up, you do have that
uplifting of your spirit.

00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:05.109
But falling in love?

00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:07.770
You’re falling off that level where you’ve
been.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.530
You’re going into a different realm.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:15.150
It’s a different world and so I think the
beauty of romantic comedies is that you make

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.840
it a little bit more palatable to take a look
at the things that happen to us.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.590
Film Courage: Now why do people love romantic
comedies?

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.870
And I realize not everybody does, but we all
have a soft spot for at least one.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.760
Pamela Jaye Smith: That’s true.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.140
We all have a soft spot for at least one.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:36.970
I will admit that when the publishers came
and we were talking about a new book, Ken

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:44.269
Lee said “Oh, there’s this study that
has been done and we think that maybe it would

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:45.269
make a good book.”

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:46.280
[And I said] “What was it?”

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.900
[Ken replied] “Couples who watch romantic
comedies together versus the other group of

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.879
couples who went to couples therapy.

00:02:53.879 --> 00:03:00.540
And an amazing thing happened, the stay-together
rate was higher for those who watched comedies

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:07.549
together and talked about them, then it was
for those who went to couples therapy.

00:03:07.549 --> 00:03:10.260
Now, I still think couples therapy is a good
idea.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.310
But until you get to that point, romantic
comedies!

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.900
Now you say “Well, does everybody like romantic
comedies?”

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.209
Frankly, I didn’t that much.

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:23.329
When Ken Lee and I were talking about this
and he said “Well, we think you would be

00:03:23.329 --> 00:03:24.819
a good person to write this book.”

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:29.290
And I said “Uh…you know I do mythology
and military stuff right and adventure?”

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.409
And he said “No…no…you’ll do a good
job with it.”

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:38.969
Well thanks to him and Michael Wiese (of Michael
Wiese productions) it was a group effort on

00:03:38.969 --> 00:03:44.920
constructing how the chapters would be laid
out and how we would approach each film.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:52.260
So me, a person who was not that much of a
rom-com fan, I actually became one through

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.439
the process of writing this book.

00:03:53.439 --> 00:03:53.790
Question for the Viewers: What is your favorite
romantic comedy?

